course: dictionary
lesson: 2957
title: Learning a new word: dromedary
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dromedary is a camel with one hump. You can find dromedaries in North Africa, the Middle East and India, and some live wild in central Australia.
explain: «At the zoo a camel kneels with its humps wobbling, and many people think the humps are water tanks.» They are not. The humps store fat, not water, so a dromedary can go for days without a drink, but the fat is food, not drink.
explain: A memory trick helps you remember the difference. A capital D lying on its side looks like a camel with one hump. A capital B has two humps, and a Bactrian camel has two.
fact: A dromedary is a camel with one hump.
fact: «Dromedary» is a noun. You can say a dromedary, or two dromedaries.
fact: The hump of a dromedary stores fat, not water.
fact: A Bactrian camel, from Central Asia, has two humps.
fact: People have kept camels to carry loads and give milk for thousands of years.
exercise: «At the zoo a camel kneels with its humps wobbling.» How many humps does a dromedary have? | *One | None | Three | Two
exercise: Which of these places is a home of the dromedary? | Antarctica | The Arctic | Iceland | *North Africa
exercise: What do the humps of a dromedary store? | Water | Sand | *Fat | Air
test: Which camel has two humps? | Dromedary | *Bactrian camel | Llama | Horse
test: What is the hump of a dromedary really for? | To hold water | *To store fat | To make a shade | To hold up its head
exam: Which memory trick helps you tell a dromedary from a Bactrian camel? | A capital B has one hump | Both letters show three humps | A capital D has two humps | *A capital D lying on its side shows one hump, and a capital B shows two
